nfirmed nothing of theirs is involved.\n- Fleet latency dashboard export with deployment annotations (telemetry): Fleet GET p99, hourly average of 5-minute p99 values (ms), night of 2–3 Nov. Annotation: 'gateway 5.8 rollout 22:05–04:10 (us-e 22:05–23:30, ap-s 23:35–01:50, eu-w 02:30–04:10)'. 20:00 90; 21:00 91; 22:00 93; 23:00 139; 00:00 284; 01:00 291; 02:00 296; 03:00 301; 04:00 298; 05:00 297. Daily median p99: 1 Nov 90, 2 Nov 91, 4 Nov 296, 6 Nov 294, 8 Nov 297. The shared change calendar for 2–3 Nov has one entry: gateway 5.8 rollout.\n- Gateway 5.8 release notes (approved 30 Oct) (change-record): Changes: (1) Fixes SEC-2291, a request-signature replay weakness reported by external auditors. Versions 5.7 and earlier remain exposed, and audit closure requires 5.8 across the fleet by 30 Nov. (2) Read path: parallel range-fetch fan-out raised from 4 to 8 for large objects. (3) TLS library upgrade. Known effect: gateway CPU +15–20% from the added signature checks. Rollback procedure: redeploy 5.7, about 4 hours across the fleet.\n- Interview notes, storage platform manager, 4 Nov (manager-account): 'We pushed a routine compaction tuning change through the fleet config system on the evening of 2 Nov, because backlog alerts had been firing since about 31 Oct. Fleet config pushes don't go on the shared change calendar and never have. Compaction is background work and yields to client reads, so it can't be behind a read-latency problem. And to be clear, we never pause compaction. Once backlog passes about 15% of extents, read amplification climbs and nodes start running toward disk-full.'\n- Network on-call handover note, 3 Nov 08:00 (staff-account): 'Elevated TCP retransmits on us-e spine-2 from 02:10 to 04:50 on 3 Nov, traced to a flapping optic. We replaced the optic at 04:50 and retransmits have been at baseline since. Nothing comparable in ap-s or eu-w. Gateway-to-storage round-trip time medians are 0.4–0.6 ms in all three regions, same as last week. We don't see a network angle on the GET latency issue.'\n- Customer success note and traffic summary, 4 Nov (document): On 30 Oct Orrin Media began migrating its archive into Tarnfield, writing about 18 TB/day across all three regions. It is a background copy with a 15 Dec deadline and is currently ahead of schedule. Orrin's contract allows temporary ingest rate limits with 24 hours' notice, and their technical lead has said pacing is fine. Fleet traffic, 28 Oct to 4 Nov: GET requests/s rose 6%, gradually across the week. PUT requests/s rose 14%.\n\nBRIEF:\nThe latency regression is not caused by gateway 5.8. It comes from the compaction tuning change pushed to storage nodes on the evening of 2 Nov. After that push, each cell's disk utilisation went from about 41% to 97% within minutes. Disk read wait rose from 9 to 205 ms, and node-side GET time rose from 61 to 262 ms. Every cell that got the push degraded within minutes, whatever its gateway version. One cell, aps-3, skipped the push, even though it had 5.8, and stayed at about 94 ms. Rolling euw-1 back to 5.7 changed nothing. Network and traffic growth don't explain it. I'm fairly sure of this, about 90%.\n\nDon't roll back the gateway. That would reopen the signature security flaw and wouldn't fix latency. Don't pause compaction, because the backlog would grow toward disk-full. Instead, revert the tuning change in one cell, watch p99 and backlog, then extend it to the other cells. Consider pacing Orrin's migration later, once the 10 Nov backlog attribution report is in. Look for a middle setting that keeps backlog under 15% without saturating disks. Fleet config pushes should also go on the shared change calendar.\n","numLines":22,"startLine":1,"totalLines":22}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01JAjFe1sc3kHmeCbVGFmQc5","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
